            <ArticleTitle>Regional versus general anesthesia for bone marrow harvesting.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>204-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To compare regional versus general anesthesia for bone marrow harvesting.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Retrospective study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Inpatients at a bone marrow transplantation unit of a university medical center.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">One hundred sixty-two donors: 63 healthy patients donating heterologous marrow, 99 patients donating autologous marrow.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Anesthesia and patient charts from 1986 to 1990 were reviewed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MEASUREMENTS AND MAIN RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Regionally anesthesized patients required less transfused blood in the perioperative period, even though more bone marrow was aspirated. This group also required less postoperative analgesia. We found no difference between the groups with regard to length of hospitalization or amount of colloid required in the perioperative period.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Our data suggest that both modes of anesthesia can be safely administered. Further investigations should be carried out to determine whether the two types of anesthesia, regional and general, are equivalent.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
